<p>Hair fall that starts suddenly, comes from all over the scalp and follows an illness, a delivery or a stressful few months is usually telogen effluvium. That is temporary, and it tends to settle on its own within months. Thinning that develops gradually in a defined pattern, such as a receding hairline, a thinning crown or a widening centre part, is usually pattern hair loss. That is progressive, and it does not reverse without treatment.</p>

<p>That rule of thumb is right often enough to be useful. It is also wrong often enough to be risky if you rely on it alone, because the two conditions overlap, one can unmask the other, and a long shed can look like thinning. The difference matters for a practical reason: waiting is the correct treatment for one and an expensive mistake for the other.</p>

<h2 id="what-telogen-effluvium-is">What telogen effluvium is</h2>

<p>Every scalp hair cycles independently through a long growth phase (anagen), a brief transition, and a resting phase (telogen), after which it falls and is replaced. At any given time most hairs are growing. Telogen effluvium happens when a shock to the system pushes a much larger share of follicles into the resting phase at once. Those hairs sit quietly for a few months, then shed together as new hairs grow in beneath them.</p>

<p>The delay is the most important clue and the most commonly missed. <a href="https://dermnetnz.org/topics/telogen-effluvium" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">DermNet's reference page on telogen effluvium</a> describes shedding becoming noticeable two to four months after the triggering event, which is why people so rarely connect the hair fall with its cause. By the time the hair comes out, the fever or the surgery feels like old news.</p>

<h3>Common triggers</h3>

<ul>
  <li>An illness with high fever, including infections common in north India such as dengue and typhoid, or COVID-19</li>
  <li>Surgery, a serious injury or a hospital admission</li>
  <li>Childbirth, with postpartum shedding typically beginning a few months after delivery</li>
  <li>Rapid weight loss, crash dieting or a sharp drop in protein intake</li>
  <li>Severe psychological stress, bereavement or a prolonged difficult period</li>
  <li>Starting, stopping or changing certain medicines, including stopping hormonal contraception</li>
  <li>Iron deficiency or a thyroid disorder, which can keep a shed going until corrected</li>
</ul>

<p>The delay also explains why hair fall can seem seasonal. Heavy shedding in the cooler months is sometimes simply the delayed shed from a fever or infection during the monsoon, arriving a few months later. Hard water and pollution tend to take the blame locally, and <a href="/blog/hard-water-pollution-and-hair-fall-in-gurgaon">what hard water, pollution and monsoon shedding really do</a> separates what they can cause from what they cannot.</p>

<h3>How it behaves</h3>

<p>The shedding is diffuse: it comes from the whole scalp rather than one area. Hair can come out in alarming amounts in the shower, on the pillow and in the brush, but the scalp rarely becomes bald. The fallen hairs usually have a small white bulb at the root, the resting club, which is normal and not a sign that the root is dying. Once the trigger has passed, DermNet describes shedding tapering back to normal over six to nine months in most cases, and the <a href="https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK430848/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">StatPearls clinical review</a> describes the outlook for recovery of density in acute telogen effluvium as good.</p>

<h2 id="what-pattern-hair-loss-is">What pattern hair loss is</h2>

<p>Pattern hair loss, or androgenetic alopecia, is a different process. It is not mainly about hairs shedding at once. Genetically susceptible follicles respond to androgens by shortening their growth phase with each cycle and producing progressively finer, shorter hairs, a change called miniaturisation. Over years, thick hairs are replaced by thinner ones, and eventually some follicles stop producing visible hair.</p>

<p>In men, it follows a recognisable sequence of hairline recession and crown thinning described by <a href="/blog/norwood-scale-explained">the Norwood scale</a>. In women, the frontal hairline is usually kept, and thinning concentrates over the crown and along a widening part; <a href="/blog/female-pattern-hair-loss-in-women">female pattern hair loss</a> has its own diagnostic considerations. In both, the course is gradual and continuous. It does not settle after six months, and it is not caused by a single event, although an event can make it more obvious.</p>

<p>Pattern loss can involve increased shedding too, which is where the confusion starts. But its defining feature is reduced hair calibre and density in a particular distribution, not the volume of hair in the drain.</p>

<h2 id="how-the-two-look-different">How the two usually look different</h2>

<p>No single feature settles it, but taken together these point one way or the other.</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Onset.</strong> Telogen effluvium tends to start abruptly, often a couple of months after a clear event. Pattern loss creeps in over years and is often spotted in photographs before it is noticed in the mirror.</li>
  <li><strong>Distribution.</strong> Effluvium thins the whole scalp fairly evenly. Pattern loss concentrates at the temples, crown or centre part, while the back and sides stay relatively full.</li>
  <li><strong>What you notice.</strong> Effluvium is noticed as hair coming out. Pattern loss is more often noticed as scalp showing through, under overhead lights, in a photograph of wet hair, or as a ponytail that feels thinner.</li>
  <li><strong>The hairs themselves.</strong> In effluvium, the shed hairs are mostly full-thickness hairs with a club root. In pattern loss, the hairs remaining in affected areas are a mix of thick hairs and noticeably fine, short ones.</li>
  <li><strong>Course.</strong> Effluvium eases over months once the trigger has gone. Pattern loss continues.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Shedding that is neither</h3>

<p>Two other conditions are worth knowing about, because they can be mistaken for either. Anagen effluvium is the rapid loss of actively growing hairs, most familiar after chemotherapy, and it begins within weeks of the cause rather than months. Diffuse alopecia areata can produce sudden, widespread thinning, sometimes with short broken hairs that are visible on trichoscopy. Both are recognised at examination and managed quite differently; <a href="/alopecia-areata-treatment-in-gurgaon">alopecia areata treatment</a> explains that pathway.</p>

<h2 id="how-a-dermatologist-tells-them-apart">How a dermatologist tells them apart</h2>

<p>The distinction is made by history and examination, not by counting hairs at home. A thorough assessment usually includes:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>A careful timeline.</strong> What happened in the two to six months before the shedding started: illness, medicines, delivery, diet, stress. This is often the single most diagnostic piece of information.</li>
  <li><strong>A gentle pull test.</strong> The doctor draws lightly on small groups of hairs in several areas. Several resting hairs coming away easily across the scalp supports an active shed.</li>
  <li><strong>Trichoscopy.</strong> Magnified examination shows whether hair diameters vary widely in one region compared with another, which is the hallmark of miniaturisation. In pure telogen effluvium, calibre is largely uniform. This is also where conditions that mimic both, such as alopecia areata or a scarring alopecia, are recognised.</li>
  <li><strong>Blood tests where indicated.</strong> Iron stores and thyroid function, and depending on the history, vitamin levels or hormone tests, to find anything that could keep a shed going. <a href="/blog/blood-tests-for-hair-fall">Which blood tests actually matter for hair fall</a> is covered separately.</li>
  <li><strong>Occasionally, a scalp biopsy.</strong> When the picture stays unclear, particularly in long-running cases, a small biopsy can show the balance of growing and resting follicles and the degree of miniaturisation.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="when-both-happen-at-once">When both happen at once</h2>

<p>This is the scenario that catches people out. A shed triggered by illness or stress can reveal pattern loss that was already developing quietly. When the effluvium settles, the hair does not return to where it was two years earlier, because the underlying pattern was progressing throughout. The stress did not cause permanent damage; it made an existing process visible sooner.</p>

<p>The opposite mistake happens too. Someone with early pattern thinning sheds heavily after a fever, is told it is just stress-related hair fall, waits a year, and loses ground they did not need to lose.</p>

<h3>Chronic telogen effluvium</h3>

<p>In some people, most often women in middle age, shedding persists or fluctuates for years without an obvious trigger. DermNet recognises this chronic form. It can closely resemble early female pattern hair loss, and the two are separated mainly on trichoscopy: persistent shedding with preserved hair calibre points to chronic effluvium, while miniaturisation points to pattern loss. That distinction changes the treatment conversation substantially.</p>

