<h2 id="when-waiting-is-right">When waiting is the right plan, and when it costs you</h2>

<p>For a clear-cut telogen effluvium, with an identified trigger that has passed, normal or corrected blood results and no sign of miniaturisation, waiting is not neglect. It is the treatment. The hair cycle needs time to reset, and injections, expensive serums or a stack of supplements do not shorten that recovery in any proven way. What helps is correcting any deficiency, gentle hair care, and standardised photographs so that recovery can actually be seen.</p>

<p>Waiting costs you when the loss is pattern thinning. Miniaturised follicles can often be maintained, and sometimes improved, with treatment, but follicles that have stopped producing visible hair for a long time are much harder to recover. Each year spent assuming the problem will resolve is a year of progression. The <a href="https://www.nhs.uk/conditions/hair-loss/" rel="noopener nofollow" target="_blank">NHS guidance on hair loss</a> notes that treatments for pattern loss work only for as long as they are used, so the earlier a decision is made, the more hair there is to keep.</p>

<p>Signs that waiting is probably not the right plan:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Shedding has continued beyond about six months with no sign of easing.</li>
  <li>The thinning is concentrated in one region, such as the crown, temples or part line, rather than spread evenly.</li>
  <li>There is a family history of similar thinning, and the change has been gradual.</li>
  <li>You notice patches, broken hairs, scaling, redness, pain or itching, which suggest a different condition altogether.</li>
  <li>The hair loss comes with other symptoms, such as fatigue, weight change, irregular periods or new facial hair.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Looking after your hair while a shed settles</h3>

<p>Washing does not cause hair loss. The hairs that come out in the shower were already loose in their follicles and would have fallen within days, so washing less often only means more come away at once, which feels worse. Gentle handling does reduce breakage: a wide-toothed comb, less heat, and fewer tight styles while the hair feels thin. Cutting hair short does not change how many follicles shed, though some people find shorter hair makes the shedding less distressing to see. Be wary of anything sold as a fix for a shed. The recovery that follows would usually have happened anyway, which is how many products acquire a reputation they do not deserve.</p>

<h2 id="why-treatment-differs">Why the treatment differs so much</h2>

<p>Because the mechanisms differ, so do the treatments. Telogen effluvium is managed by finding and removing the trigger, correcting any deficiency and allowing time. Pattern loss is managed with treatments that act on miniaturisation, most commonly minoxidil and, in men, finasteride. Both need ongoing use and have side effects worth discussing properly, which <a href="/blog/minoxidil-and-finasteride-in-india">minoxidil and finasteride in India</a> sets out. Adjuncts are sometimes added, although <a href="/blog/does-prp-work-for-hair-loss">the evidence for PRP is modest</a> and it works as maintenance rather than a cure.</p>

<p>For established thin or bald areas in stable pattern loss, <a href="/hair-transplant-in-gurgaon">a hair transplant</a> may be considered. It does nothing for a shed, and active shedding is one of the situations described in <a href="/blog/who-should-not-have-a-hair-transplant">when a hair transplant is the wrong answer</a>, because nobody can judge the true extent of the loss while hair is still coming out.</p>

<p>Getting the diagnosis wrong in either direction is costly. Someone with effluvium who is put on long-term medication takes drugs they may not need. Someone with pattern loss who is told to wait loses time they cannot recover.</p>

<h2 id="what-to-do-now">What to do now</h2>

<ol>
  <li>Write down when the shedding started and what happened in the months before it.</li>
  <li>Take photographs in consistent light from the front, the top and the crown, and repeat them monthly.</li>
  <li>Avoid starting several products at once, which makes it impossible to know what helped.</li>
  <li>Get the relevant blood tests rather than guessing with supplements.</li>
  <li>Have the scalp examined if shedding persists beyond a few months, or sooner if the thinning follows a pattern.</li>
</ol>

